[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: StrictDoc mutates a loaded index in place, unguarded and without rollback

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[EVIDENCE]
UID: EV-STRICTDOC-MUTATION-PRIMITIVES
TITLE: StrictDoc mutates a loaded index in place, unguarded and without rollback
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
SOURCE: audit plus refute-and-defend contest over strictdoc 0.28.3 driven against a corpus copy; 2026-08-31
STATEMENT: >>>
An audit of strictdoc 0.28.3 followed by an adversarial refute-and-defend pair,
all three driving the code against a copy of this repository's corpus.

Settled by both sides. The TraceabilityIndex mutation primitives run headlessly
with no HTTP layer. SDWriter and SDReader are index-independent.
CreateOrUpdateNodeCommand os.utime()s every document -- 348 of 348 from a no-op
edit -- while the primitives touch 0 or 2. update_requirement_parent_uid writes
the graph only, so the writer emits a byte-identical file and the edge is lost
on reload. StrictDoc's cycle detection is inert on a role-bearing corpus: the
facade defaults edge=None while all 869 relations carry roles, and both agents
created a real cycle with no exception. There is no transaction or rollback
anywhere, and invariants are bare asserts.

Contested and unresolved at the headline. The refuter drove a colliding UID
through the primitives and got a half-applied index -- fields already cleared,
old UID link deleted, nothing to roll back. The defender answered with
create_from_document_tree: the same code a full reload runs, minus the file
walk, at 20 ms against 500 ms, catching duplicate UID, dangling reference and
missing required field when driven at each.

Both hold. The primitives half-apply; a 20 ms whole-graph rebuild makes that
recoverable. The design takes both findings rather than either verdict.
<<<
